<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sessho-and-kampaku">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sesshō and Kampaku</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sekkan, Sesshō, Kampaku, Taikō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sesshō and Kampaku were the two regencies of the Japanese court, together called sekkan: the sesshō acted for a child emperor or an empress regnant, the kampaku assisted an adult emperor as chief adviser and regent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-yoshifusa">Fujiwara no Yoshifusa</a> became the first sesshō from outside the imperial house in 858, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-mototsune">Fujiwara no Mototsune</a> the first kampaku in 887. Marrying their daughters to emperors, the Fujiwara monopolised both offices from the 10th century and dominated the Heian court, until the cloistered rule of retired emperors from the 11th century left the titles without real power. Held in rotation by the five regent houses, they were abolished in 1868.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In earlier times only members of the imperial family could act as sesshō. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a> reports that Emperor Ōjin was assisted by his mother, Empress Jingū, though it is doubtful whether this is historical; the first historical sesshō was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku">Prince Shōtoku</a>, who assisted Empress Suiko. The regents of the Heian court came from the Fujiwara Hokke, the northern branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-yoshifusa">Fujiwara no Yoshifusa</a> belonged. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-mototsune">Fujiwara no Mototsune</a>, the first kampaku, was his nephew and adopted son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The regents carried to the emperor the policies drawn up by the Sadaijin, the Minister of the Left, and other senior officials of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daijo-kan">Daijō-kan</a>, the Council of State, and carried the emperor's decisions back to them. They sometimes decided on the emperor's behalf, yet their offices were not defined by law and carried no specific political authority. Like imperial princes and princesses, both were styled denka, Highness. Power under the Fujiwara reached its height in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-michinaga">Fujiwara no Michinaga</a> and Fujiwara no Yorimichi.</p><p>The five regent houses, the Konoe, Kujō, Ichijō, Takatsukasa and Nijō families, descended from Yorimichi; the Konoe and Kujō through Fujiwara no Tadamichi, the others from those two. Until 1868 they held the titles alone, with two exceptions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> became the first kampaku not noble by birth, by having himself adopted into the Konoe family, and his nephew Toyotomi Hidetsugu followed him. A retired kampaku was called Taikō, a title that came to mean Hideyoshi himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Although the old offices were abolished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, the office of sesshō was set down again in the Imperial Household Law of 1889 and in the new law of 1948, both of which restrict it to members of the imperial family. Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a>, later Emperor Shōwa, served as sesshō from 1921 to 1926 for Emperor Taishō and was called sesshō-no-miya, the Prince-Regent. The Taikō district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>, with its main street Taikō-dōri, takes its name from Hideyoshi's title.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sesshō and Kampaku” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 858 Fujiwara no Yoshifusa became the first man outside the imperial family to act as regent for a child emperor, his grandson; his adopted son Mototsune was made <b>kampaku</b>, regent for an adult emperor, a newly invented post.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>He began Osaka castle as his base, became imperial regent (<b>kampaku</b>) in 1585 and received the new surname Toyotomi in 1586.</blockquote><a href="/state/toyotomi-regime">Toyotomi regime</a></li></ul></section>
